<omdoc base="latin:/fundamentals/relations.omdoc"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/relations.mmt#0.0.0:1999.86.0"/><meta property="http://cds.omdoc.org/?metadata?importedby"><om:OMOBJ xmlns:om="http://www.openmath.org/OpenMath"><om:OMLIT type="http://www.openmath.org/cd?OpenMath?OMSTR" value="mmt-omdoc"/></om:OMOBJ></meta></metadata><instruction text="namespace latin:/"/><instruction text="fixmeta http://cds.omdoc.org/urtheories?PLF"/><mref name="[latin:/?OneTyped]" target="latin:/?OneTyped"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/relations.mmt#38.4.0:52.4.14"/></metadata></mref><mref name="[latin:/?Relation]" target="latin:/?Relation"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/relations.mmt#78.8.0:92.8.14"/></metadata></mref><mref name="[latin:/?Reflexivity]" target="latin:/?Reflexivity"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/relations.mmt#184.13.0:201.13.17"/></metadata></mref><mref name="[latin:/?Symmetry]" target="latin:/?Symmetry"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/relations.mmt#247.18.0:261.18.14"/></metadata></mref><mref name="[latin:/?Transitivity]" target="latin:/?Transitivity"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/relations.mmt#314.23.0:332.23.18"/></metadata></mref><mref name="[latin:/?Preorder]" target="latin:/?Preorder"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/relations.mmt#612.32.0:626.32.14"/></metadata></mref><mref name="[latin:/?EquivalenceRelation]" target="latin:/?EquivalenceRelation"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/relations.mmt#682.37.0:707.37.25"/></metadata></mref><mref name="[latin:/?Congruence]" target="latin:/?Congruence"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/relations.mmt#756.42.0:772.42.16"/></metadata></mref><mref name="[latin:/?EquivalenceCongruence]" target="latin:/?EquivalenceCongruence"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/relations.mmt#868.47.0:895.47.27"/></metadata></mref><opaque format="text"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/relations.mmt#957.52.0:1319.54.181"/></metadata>Equality is an equivalence-congruence.
   But semantic equality is even stronger: it allows substitution in objects of any type so that equal objects can never be distinguished.
   This is different from the syntactic equality used inside a logic, where equal objets cannot be distinguished by logical formulas but might be distinguishable by other judgments.</opaque><mref name="[latin:/?SemanticEquality]" target="latin:/?SemanticEquality"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/relations.mmt#1321.55.0:1343.55.22"/></metadata></mref><mref name="[latin:/?EqualityType]" target="latin:/?EqualityType"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/relations.mmt#1588.63.0:1606.63.18"/></metadata></mref><mref name="[latin:/?AntiSymmetry]" target="latin:/?AntiSymmetry"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/relations.mmt#1828.76.0:1846.76.18"/></metadata></mref><mref name="[latin:/?Order]" target="latin:/?Order"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/relations.mmt#1936.82.0:1947.82.11"/></metadata></mref></omdoc>